00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:49.270
right?

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:51.840
You know, the package of ideas that I'm talking
about.

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:58.330
One, are those people that would even conceivably
say this forward party idea that Andrew Yang

00:14:58.330 --> 00:15:00.630
proposes in a coalition with the left makes
sense.

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:04.040
And number two, are these even people that
you want as part of your coalition?

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:08.420
Speaker 4: First, let me say, I want everyone
as part of the coalition.

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:11.300
You know, if you can vote, we can disagree
on a lot of things.

00:15:11.300 --> 00:15:12.300
It's all to the good.

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:16.860
If we agree on the fact that this duopoly
is now working, it's designed to turn us against

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:18.440
each other and sink us.

00:15:18.440 --> 00:15:22.050
So that that's one of the principles of the
Foreign Party is that, look, I can disagree

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:25.010
with you on a lot of stuff, but we can make
common cause on this.

00:15:25.010 --> 00:15:28.800
Then we can go back to disagreeing when the
democracy is actually set up to succeed when

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:29.900
they're, you know.

00:15:29.900 --> 00:15:31.970
Speaker 1: So let me, I mean, let me ask.

00:15:31.970 --> 00:15:38.240
So even I mean to to make it really specific
if people on the right say the Republican

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:43.910
Party is too centrist, I'm an extreme white
supremacist or something like that.

00:15:43.910 --> 00:15:48.170
But I think we need to get, you know, we need
single transferable vote or whatever and campaign

00:15:48.170 --> 00:15:49.300
you.

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:52.470
You still welcome that because you're on the
same page on that one issue.

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:57.610
Speaker 4: Yeah, if they're going to help
us transition to a more effective democracy.

00:15:57.610 --> 00:16:02.600
You know, I don't need to know what their
stance is on things that I disagree with.

00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:04.670
People have a sense of where I'm coming from.

00:16:04.670 --> 00:16:05.670
OK.

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:11.920
But right now, I believe that there is an
existential threat to the country as a result

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:15.990
of the greatest design flaw in the history
of the world.

00:16:15.990 --> 00:16:21.700
And if you will help us resolve that design
flaw, then we can have a discussion about

00:16:21.700 --> 00:16:26.510
anything else under the Sun at a time when
the democracy actually will stand the test

00:16:26.510 --> 00:16:32.560
of time when we transition to a multi-party
system where you can't make any autocratic

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:36.910
or authoritarian decisions, you have to somehow
get two or three other parties on board.

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:38.300
Speaker 1: OK, all right, fair.

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:41.350
So on the question of is everyone literally
welcome?

00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:43.280
The answer is yes, that's that's fine.

00:16:43.280 --> 00:16:44.280
That's legitimate.

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:45.560
So that's clear.

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:53.380
Now, in terms of to whom that currently typically
votes for Republicans, would this appeal?

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:54.430
Where are you on that?

00:16:54.430 --> 00:16:59.050
Speaker 4: There are all sorts of folks who
are displeased with Trump.

00:16:59.050 --> 00:17:02.510
There's a person on the line who said the
other day, it's like, Look, I voted for Trump

00:17:02.510 --> 00:17:06.139
twice, and I don't even want him back.

00:17:06.139 --> 00:17:12.439
You know, like they're like their voters out
there who are concerned about the country.

00:17:12.439 --> 00:17:19.220
And I've been heartened by the fact that I've
gotten a lot of incoming expressions of support

00:17:19.220 --> 00:17:26.439
from military veterans, members of law enforcement
or former law enforcement who despair about

00:17:26.439 --> 00:17:27.439
the state of the country.

00:17:27.439 --> 00:17:31.970
And they see where it's going to, and they
say, this is what I've been waiting for.

00:17:31.970 --> 00:17:38.029
There's an independent community that right
now is politically homeless.

00:17:38.029 --> 00:17:39.029
They're very numerous.

00:17:39.029 --> 00:17:41.160
There are 44 percent of Americans.

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:47.370
Consider themselves independents, and I would
say that we're seeing a lot of them come in

00:17:47.370 --> 00:17:49.340
like one of the things I'm going to suggest.

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:53.200
First, if you look at the appetite for a third
party, it's higher among both Republicans

00:17:53.200 --> 00:17:54.659
and independents and Democrats.

00:17:54.659 --> 00:17:59.940
And so if you imagine who's going to draw
third party energy like we're where is going

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:01.350
to come from.

00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:08.070
Like by the numbers, it's likely to come from
those groups and imagining that every Republican

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:13.570
or former Republican or independent is excited
about Trump is just not right.

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:19.210
You know, but but saying that that group is
also open to Democrats is also not right.

00:18:19.210 --> 00:18:24.770
I have encountered so many Americans who just
shut down when you have a D next to your name.

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:25.770
Sure.

00:18:25.770 --> 00:18:26.770
Which I found unfortunate.

00:18:26.770 --> 00:18:30.490
But the thing I want to suggest to people
is that, again, all politics is tribal at

00:18:30.490 --> 00:18:31.980
this point.

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:36.760
And so what we need to do is we need to have
more tribes emerge that we have a chance.

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:39.140
If we stick to two tribes, we're not.

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:42.760
Speaker 1: Last thing I want to ask you about
and we're shifting gears a little bit, but

00:18:42.760 --> 00:18:46.610
because your background in technology, I'm
very curious as to your insights, particularly

00:18:46.610 --> 00:18:51.490
as someone who who used social media really
effectively in the Democratic primary run

00:18:51.490 --> 00:18:57.309
that you made all the talk now about the so-called
regulation of social media.

00:18:57.309 --> 00:19:03.640
One interesting artifact of this has been
the rapidity with which those who have for

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:07.650
a long time claimed businesses should be able
to do whatever they want as long as it's not

00:19:07.650 --> 00:19:08.650
illegal.

00:19:08.650 --> 00:19:14.139
Are now demanding that social media companies
be forced to allow messages that violate their

00:19:14.139 --> 00:19:15.990
terms of service to be up.

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:19.289
That's a hypocrisy we could talk about, but
I don't want to focus on that necessarily

00:19:19.289 --> 00:19:20.289
today.

00:19:20.289 --> 00:19:24.669
What I'm curious about from your perspective
is what should be the regulatory infrastructure

00:19:24.669 --> 00:19:29.759
as to the social networks, particularly from
this point of view of political bias that

00:19:29.759 --> 00:19:35.320
Don Jr., whoever else, are regularly tweeting
about and sort of saying, We are the victims

00:19:35.320 --> 00:19:36.320
here.

00:19:36.320 --> 00:19:37.320
What what would you like to see?

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:43.470
Speaker 4: I think our government needs to
modernize its approach to social media and

00:19:43.470 --> 00:19:44.760
technology in general.

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:46.370
Right now, it's behind the curve.

00:19:46.370 --> 00:19:49.140
It's laughable or embarrassing.

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:51.730
This is also an outgrowth of our Jarrin autocracy,
David.

00:19:51.730 --> 00:19:56.940
I mean, the reality is that our congressional
leaders are in their 70s or 80s.

00:19:56.940 --> 00:20:01.310
If it is Biden vs. Trump in 2004, which it
looks like it's going in that direction, their

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:03.399
combined age will be one hundred and fifty
eight.

00:20:03.399 --> 00:20:04.399
Yeah.

00:20:04.399 --> 00:20:08.259
So then asking our government to understand
what's going on in social media is at least

00:20:08.259 --> 00:20:12.100
right now, a bridge too far and like that
has to change.

00:20:12.100 --> 00:20:19.850
If I were in charge, I would be getting into
the guts of these social media companies,

00:20:19.850 --> 00:20:25.029
setting what Jonathan Height suggested as
like a filter where you end up being able

00:20:25.029 --> 00:20:29.640
to just like, ignore people who are customarily
aggressive or obnoxious.

00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:34.509
I mean, you can watch it if you want or get
those messages if you want, but you actually

00:20:34.509 --> 00:20:40.470
set up a way to reward people who tend to
be more balanced and normal in their conversation.

00:20:40.470 --> 00:20:46.520
OK, now the energy and social media rewards
the more aggressive or sensationalistic yourself.

00:20:46.520 --> 00:20:50.009
Except it again, you just like, keep it, keep
it even keeled.

00:20:50.009 --> 00:20:51.139
And yet everyone loves you.

00:20:51.139 --> 00:20:53.049
Speaker 1: Well, you should see the emails
I get.

00:20:53.049 --> 00:20:57.230
But so it sounds like you're not saying you
would be in favor of the government going

00:20:57.230 --> 00:21:01.000
to YouTube and saying you've got to live up
anti-vaccine content.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:06.950
Speaker 4: Oh, there there are certain things
that you'd have to say are in the public good

00:21:06.950 --> 00:21:16.360
that, you know, and there's disinformation
and there's disinformation that can be destructive

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:22.020
in a way that, you know, like if you are functioning
society, you do not want to say like every

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:27.419
idea is the same, including ideas that, by
the way, in this case could lead people to

00:21:27.419 --> 00:21:28.419
lose their lives.

00:21:28.419 --> 00:21:34.740
Speaker 1: So, so in other words, you want
to make it easier to control one's experience

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:40.389
on these platforms, but not take the approach
that some on the right are saying, which is

00:21:40.389 --> 00:21:45.519
these these platforms should be forced to
allow content that violates their terms of

00:21:45.519 --> 00:21:51.289
service again, as long as it's not illegal
on the basis of discrimination on race or

00:21:51.289 --> 00:21:52.570
sex or this type of thing.

00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:56.299
Speaker 4: Well, I want to echo what some
of the social media CEOs themselves say, which

00:21:56.299 --> 00:21:59.940
is like, why are we in a position to make
this decision?

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:03.610
And the reason they're making that decision
is because the government is absent.

00:22:03.610 --> 00:22:11.190
One of the social media CEOs said, Look, we
should get together tech, government, media,

00:22:11.190 --> 00:22:13.340
nonprofits and set rules of the road.

00:22:13.340 --> 00:22:17.590
So it's not just us doing the content moderation
decision and you yelling at us about it because

00:22:17.590 --> 00:22:20.690
wherever they draw the line, they're going
to get yelled at.

00:22:20.690 --> 00:22:25.129
You know, I happen to think the Conservatives
are off base in terms of saying like, Oh,

00:22:25.129 --> 00:22:27.529
you know, you're sledging conservative content.

00:22:27.529 --> 00:22:31.499
It's like conservative content actually performs
great on a lot of these platforms.

00:22:31.499 --> 00:22:35.730
And if there's anything we should be concerned
about, it's the deteriorating mental health

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:39.340
of our young people, teenage girls in particular.

00:22:39.340 --> 00:22:43.050
So if you're a conservative, I get mad about
that in my mind, right?

00:22:43.050 --> 00:22:44.050
Yeah.

00:22:44.050 --> 00:22:47.460
Being depressed, like, isn't that a bigger
issue than the fact that, you know, anyway?

00:22:47.460 --> 00:22:52.720
Speaker 1: And I guess then the question would
become if Facebook knew that was going on

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:55.120
and was hiding it for a period of time.

00:22:55.120 --> 00:22:59.910
Are they really good faith players to be involved
in figuring out the regulation, which is sort

00:22:59.910 --> 00:23:01.039
of a second order question?

00:23:01.039 --> 00:23:05.200
Speaker 4: Well, they can be at the table,
but we should stop pretending that companies

00:23:05.200 --> 00:23:08.500
are going to do anything other than what's
going to maximize their financial returns,

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:10.570
which is exactly what you'd expect them to
do.

00:23:10.570 --> 00:23:11.570
So.

00:23:11.570 --> 00:23:14.230
So if you have that expectation and then you
go in and say, Hey, guess what?

00:23:14.230 --> 00:23:18.470
We're going to do things that are going to
make your financial returns lower and tough

00:23:18.470 --> 00:23:19.470
luck, you know?

00:23:19.470 --> 00:23:24.330
And so one thing I suggested as like a random
thing, it's like maybe Instagram's a turnoff

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:28.669
for teenagers between midnight and 7:00 a.m.
like, you know, maybe just looking at pictures

00:23:28.669 --> 00:23:32.831
of your high school classmates or something
we don't want you to do in the middle of the

00:23:32.831 --> 00:23:37.139
night, you know, like, you know, just like
a random thought.

00:23:37.139 --> 00:23:40.309
And so you can see that kind of like with
Facebook, we're excited about that.

00:23:40.309 --> 00:23:41.309
Of course not.

00:23:41.309 --> 00:23:43.149
They'd be like, Oh, we make money, then we
make money all the time, right?

00:23:43.149 --> 00:23:48.890
You know, hopefully
Speaker 1: the book is forward notes on the

00:23:48.890 --> 00:23:49.890
future of our democracy.

00:23:49.890 --> 00:23:55.100
I really appreciate reading the book, and
we've been speaking with the book's author,

00:23:55.100 --> 00:23:56.100
Andrew Yang.

00:23:56.100 --> 00:23:58.370
Always great having you on, and I really appreciate
your time.

00:23:58.370 --> 00:23:59.710
Speaker 4: Thank you, Dave.

00:23:59.710 --> 00:24:00.309
It's great to be back.

